This was a hobbit monk, and was pretty straightforward. The maximum of
18 on strength was a little restrictive until I got a blessed bag of
holding, but the 20 constitution made up for it. This guy practically
regenerated naturally (it looks like Death drained a point there and I
didn't even notice it...). He wasn't so hot with spells, probably
because of the maximum 16 intelligence. By the time I got up to 20
charisma, this attribute was already a moot point. My real maximum
hitpoints (before scrapping mano a deadly mano twice with Death) was
350.

Minetown had a neutral priest and I left him alone. (Came back for
protection a time or two.) I then did Sokoban and got the amulet of
reflection, and found a bag of holding on the way (yay!). I went back
down and did the Gnome King's Wine Cellar and killed Ruggo. The rest
was pretty straightforward.

Techniques really make monks powerful. I took Master Kaen out with one
shot from a wand of fire to wake him, and one chained blitz to take him
down. I was 12th level at the time. Whenever I'd get nervous about a
monster, I'd do the chained blitz thing and seldom actually get through
to the ground slam at the end before it fell. (Master Kaen died in the
crater, leaving the dangerous job of getting out of a pit while a horde
of elementals closes in.)

I waited to take on Vecna until after I had genocided Ls, so that was a
pretty easy quest. (There I had the disaster that he stumbled backward
during my chained blitz, so I didn't get to finish it. But the residual
chi-strike took him out with one more hit.) There was a nymph level,
but no rats or kobolds, a guild of disgruntled adventurers but no big
level. If Fort Ludios existed, I didn't find it, not that I looked too
too hard. I went into Sam's and coaxed his pets to kill one another,
but then realized that I (1) didn't have a wand of teleport and (2)
didn't really need anything, so what was the point of stealing from
Sam... I forgot that I had a ring of conflict on, though, and almost
lost the game to Thiefbane (just lost a level, stepped back and removed
the ring). I bought a few identify scrolls off of him for 1000 gp each,
since I didn't yet have a magic marker.

Throughout Gehennom I was carrying 9 gp visibly. So I got up to Geryon,
and apparently he had heard what had happened to Orcus (down before he
could teleport away), Demogorgon (stoned by a rubber chicken), Juiblex
(didn't even swallow me) and Yeenoghu (summoned by Juiblex and somewhat
more successful -- he escaped upstairs before dying), because he
demanded 1 gold piece for safe passage. I offered him zero, and he got
offended. Go figure. Dispater wanted 1 gold and I refused him as well.
Asmodeus (being a super tough mega-devil) demanded a whole 3 gold
pieces. I tried to insult him by offering one, but he took the gold and
cut his losses. Baalzebub demanded 2 and took one.

Moloch's Sanctum almost killed me. After killing Rodney at the
entrance, I made the mistake of playing with a gypsy, who promptly
summoned him again. A couple of love taps sent him away and I didn't
see him for a while. When I got to the inner sanctum, I found out why.
Rodney was trying to kill the high priest to get the Amulet himself.
Unfortunately, he wasn't distracted enough by his battle to forget to
summon pets 3 or four times. I was down to 68 hitpoints by the time I
escaped with the amulet, having killed Rodney and his Double-trouble
brother. The minions sent by my god were very helpful in all this,
especially the Planetar. (There was also a Ki-rin, whom I had left
behind before entering Gehennom, but who rejoined me for the elemental
planes.)

I was surprised to see that my Ki-rin and my Planetar followed me into
the elemental planes. This made things much easier, as they ran
interference for me. Unfortunately, something killed the Ki-Rin on the
plane of Fire, so only the Planetar followed me to the Astral Plane,
where he was joined by my guardian angel. I had to visit all three
temples. First up was the chaotic temple, guarded by Pestilence. The
Planetar took him down once, and I killed him once. Then the neutral,
guarded by Death. I very nearly lost the game here (amulet of
life-saving to the rescue). On the way in, I avoided Death completely,
but the ice mage who was wandering around in there summoned lots of
high-level nasties, who managed to kill my guardian angel, and me to
boot. On the way out, I was forced to face Death, who only required two
hits to kill, but this enabled him to claw me twice, draining my max
hitpoints. He resurrected before I could get through the door, which
was perpetually blocked by insects (it didn't occur to me at this point
to use a teleportation wand) and again clawed my twice.

Finally the lawful temple, which was guarded by Famine. I had invoked
"Blink" for the first time in the game to ensure my getaway from Death,
so I got three moves on Famine with no response from him (except for
dying on the third blow). Approaching the temple, I remembered my wand
of teleport, cleared the door, went to the altar, blew my magic
whistle, and ascended.

